theorem measurable_sample_of_lt_prefixFiltration
    {Ω S : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] [MeasurableSpace S]
    (ξ : ℕ → Ω → S) (hξ : ∀ n, Measurable (ξ n))
    {n i : ℕ} (hi : i < n) :
    Measurable[(SOptLib.filtration ξ hξ).seq n] (ξ i) := by
  refine Measurable.of_comap_le ?_
  unfold SOptLib.filtration
  exact le_iSup_of_le i (le_iSup_of_le hi le_rfl)

/-- A finite sample-block sigma-algebra generated by measurable coordinates is
below the ambient measurable space.

For an abstract sample family indexed by `I`, if every coordinate in a finite
block is measurable for the ambient measurable space on `Omega`, then the
finite-block measurable space generated by that `Finset I` is a sub-sigma-
algebra of the ambient space.

Layer: Model | Gap: Level 0 (finite sample-block ambient sub-sigma-algebra)
Proof: unfold the finite-block sigma-algebra as an indexed supremum of coordinate
  comaps, then bound each generator by ambient measurability and close by
  `iSup_le`.
Source: Mathlib measure-theory measurable-space comap and complete-lattice APIs
Used in: stochastic conditional-gradient finite sample-block measurability and
  stochastic mirror descent finite mini-batch history measurability
Book citation: book/FOML/StochasticNonconvexConditionalGradient.json#/algorithm_spec
Origin algorithm: Lan, First-order and Stochastic Optimization Methods for
  Machine Learning, stochastic nonconvex conditional gradient -/
theorem sampleBlockMeasurableSpace_le
    {Omega Xi I : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Omega] [MeasurableSpace Xi]
    (xi : I → Omega → Xi) (block : Finset I)
    (hxi : ∀ i, i ∈ block → Measurable (xi i)) :
    sampleBlockMeasurableSpace xi block ≤
      (by infer_instance : MeasurableSpace Omega) := by
  rw [sampleBlockMeasurableSpace]
  exact iSup_le fun q => (hxi q.1 q.2).comap_le

/-- Finite sample-block sigma-algebras are monotone in the coordinate block.

If every coordinate index in `A` is also in `B`, then the sigma-algebra generated
by the sample coordinates in `A` is below the sigma-algebra generated by the
coordinates in `B`.

Layer: Model | Gap: Level 0 (finite sample-block filtration monotonicity)
Proof: unfold `sampleBlockMeasurableSpace` and include each generator from the
  smaller subtype-indexed supremum into the larger one using the block inclusion.
Source: Mathlib measure-theory measurable-space comap and complete-lattice APIs
Used in: nonconvex stochastic conditional gradient enlargement of finite sample
  footprints for iterate and oracle-estimator measurability
Book citation: book/FOML/NonconvexStochasticConditionalGradient.json#/algorithm_spec
Origin algorithm: Lan, First-order and Stochastic Optimization Methods for
  Machine Learning, stochastic nonconvex conditional gradient -/
theorem sampleBlockMeasurableSpace_mono
    {Ω Ξ I : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ξ]
    (ξ : I → Ω → Ξ) {A B : Finset I} (hAB : A ⊆ B) :
    sampleBlockMeasurableSpace ξ A ≤ sampleBlockMeasurableSpace ξ B := by
  classical
  rw [sampleBlockMeasurableSpace, sampleBlockMeasurableSpace]
  exact iSup_le (fun q =>
    le_iSup_of_le (⟨q.1, hAB q.2⟩ : {q // q ∈ B}) le_rfl)

